    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_95b41daa",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: skunk_meme; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a skunk in a grassy field with overlaid text that reads, 'Imagine being so disgusting there have to be laws to try to stop normal people from hating you.' The meme uses the skunk as a visual metaphor for something inherently unpleasant or offensive, implying that its very existence causes discomfort or hatred in others. The intent is to humorously or sarcastically suggest that the skunk is so reviled that it requires legal protection from public disdain. While not targeting a specific human group, the meme relies on the skunk's natural odor as a source of disgust, which can be interpreted as a form of biological or animal-based ridicule. This can be considered harmful because it reinforces negative associations with a living creature, potentially contributing to the devaluation or stigmatization of animals perceived as 'dirty' or 'unpleasant.' The meme's intent is to provoke laughter through the absurdity of legal protection for a naturally offensive animal, but it may also normalize the idea that certain beings are 'too disgusting' to coexist peacefully with humans, which can subtly reinforce negative attitudes toward animals or even serve as a metaphor for human social dynamics.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_304e2024"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
